<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- CFBundleDisplayName is deliberately absent: it comes from
       INFOPLIST_KEY_CFBundleDisplayName in project.yml, which is the single place the
       user-facing name is written. A literal here would be a second copy that nothing
       keeps in step, and this is the string a person actually reads — it names the
       keyboard in Settings and on the globe key. -->
  <!-- Export compliance, declared by Jonah on 2026-09-05: the app uses no encryption.
       It contains no cryptography and does no networking at all — no CryptoKit, no
       CommonCrypto, no Security framework, no URLSession, no third-party dependency, and
       the keyboard has no Full Access so iOS would not let it reach the network anyway.
       Answering it here rather than in App Store Connect is why the question stops being
       asked on every upload. Do not delete this as unexplained: without it a build uploads
       successfully and is then never distributed to any tester, which is a failure that
       looks like success. -->
  <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
  <false/>
  <!-- CFBundleName is deliberately not set here. It cannot be: with
       GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE on, Xcode merges its own keys over this file and always
       writes CFBundleName = $(PRODUCT_NAME), so a $(PRODUCT_DISPLAY_NAME) written here
       looked right in the file and produced "KeyBored" in two uploaded builds.
       INFOPLIST_KEY_CFBundleName is not a setting Xcode honours either; both were tried
       on 2026-09-05 and read back out of the archived product.

       So CFBundleName tracks the target name and stays KeyBored, which is consistent
       with SPEC.md section 10: PRODUCT_NAME also names the Swift module, the executable
       and the test host, all of which are internal. Setting it to the brand was tried
       and reverted — it broke `@testable import KeyBored` in every test file, which is
       the internal name leaking into source for the sake of one manifest key.

       This is only safe because CFBundleName is a fallback for when CFBundleDisplayName
       does not fit, and "BoreKey" is seven characters against a fifteen-character
       budget. Not verified on a device: if the old name ever shows up in Settings, this
       is the key it came from. -->
  <key>NSExtension</key>
  <dict>
    <key>NSExtensionAttributes</key>
    <dict>
      <key>IsASCIICapable</key>
      <false/>
      <key>PrefersRightToLeft</key>
      <false/>
      <key>PrimaryLanguage</key>
      <string>en-US</string>
      <key>RequestsOpenAccess</key>
      <false/>
    </dict>
    <key>NSExtensionPointIdentifier</key>
    <string>com.apple.keyboard-service</string>
    <key>NSExtensionPrincipalClass</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME).KeyboardViewController</string>
  </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
